body:
`update-user` looks up the stored record by `updated.email` after merging the request body into the session user. If the request updates `email`, this will (a) fail to update the original stored user (still keyed by the old email) and (b) can accidentally mutate a different user if `updated.email` matches an existing account. Consider either disallowing email changes in this mock handler or re-keying the `users` map from the previous email to the new email before applying updates.
```suggestion
      const previousEmail = currentSession.user.email;
      const requestedEmail = body.email ?? previousEmail;

      // Prevent accidentally overwriting a different user when changing email
      if (requestedEmail !== previousEmail && users.has(requestedEmail)) {
        return HttpResponse.json(
          { message: 'Email already in use' },
          { status: 400 },
        );
      }

      const { email: _ignoredEmail, ...restBody } = body;
      const updated: MockUser = {
        ...currentSession.user,
        ...restBody,
        email: requestedEmail,
      };
      currentSession = { ...currentSession, user: updated };

      // Sync the stored record if we have one, re-keying if email changed
      const stored = users.get(previousEmail);
      if (stored) {
        Object.assign(stored, restBody, { email: requestedEmail });
        if (requestedEmail !== previousEmail) {
          users.delete(previousEmail);
          users.set(requestedEmail, stored);
        }
```
